questions.question:
What is the output of the following C program?

#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int x = 5, y = 9;
    x = (x = x + y) - (y = x - y);
    printf("%d %d ", x, y);
    return 0;
}

What is the output of the following C program?

#include <stdio.h>
int main() {
    int i = 5;
    printf("%d %d %d", i++, i, ++i);
    return 0;
}

Consider the following recursive function in C:

int function(int x, int y) {
    if (y <= 0) return x;
    return function(y, x % y);
}

What does this recursive function compute?

Assume a 64-bit operating system. A developer declares two variables:
I. int (*ptr)[3];
II. int *arr[3];
What are the memory sizes allocated to 'ptr' and 'arr' respectively?

Consider a 32-bit system (where packing/padding is ignored). A structure and union are declared as:

struct MyStruct { char a; int b; float c; };
union MyUnion { char a; int b; float c; };

What are the memory sizes of MyStruct and MyUnion respectively?

What is the output of the following recursive C program?

#include <stdio.h>
void count(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) return;
    printf("%d ", n);
    count(n - 1);
    printf("%d ", n);
}
int main() {
    count(3);
    return 0;
}
